{
  "teamId": 108,
  "year": 2021,
  "narrative": "The 2021 Los Angeles Angels were a team caught perpetually between promise and disappointment, never quite able to stitch together enough consistency to overcome a rotation and bullpen that bled runs at an alarming rate. They opened the year in deceptive equilibrium — a 12-12 April that masked a bullpen already cracking under pressure, with costly home runs surrendered by Raisel Iglesias and Mike Mayers punctuating what should have been winning moments. The presence of Shohei Ohtani, embarking on one of the most remarkable two-way seasons in baseball history, and the steady production of Jared Walsh gave fans genuine reasons to believe.\n\nMay brought the Angels crashing back to earth. A 12-18 record made it the worst month of the season, and the bullpen continued to be the team's undoing. Mayers and Iglesias were torched repeatedly in high-leverage spots, surrendering leads that the offense worked hard to build. Yet even in the darkness, Ohtani's moonshot against Boston — a go-ahead two-run blast that carried a 0.727 WPA, the single most impactful positive play of the entire season — reminded everyone what this team could be when its superstar was locked in.\n\nJune and July told a more hopeful story. Back-to-back winning months at 15-11 and 13-11 suggested the Angels had steadied the ship, with clutch hits from Luis Rengifo, Jared Walsh, and others providing the timely offense the team needed. But the bullpen never truly reformed — Iglesias gave up leads on the Fourth of July and in late July, and the team's inability to close out games remained a structural flaw that no hot streak could paper over indefinitely.\n\nAugust was a tease and September was a collapse. The offense flashed real power — Jo Adell's grand slam, Max Stassi's big shot, Jack Mayfield's bases-clearing double in late September — but the rotation posted a 4.69 ERA on the year and the bullpen continued to fail in the most important moments. A 9-17 September effectively ended any lingering playoff hopes, and even a 2-1 October finish couldn't disguise what had become a familiar Angels story: a magnificent Ohtani performance surrounded by a team that simply wasn't good enough to carry him to October baseball.",
  "chapters": [
